  "live_records": {
    "q-132463": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132463",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time between customer calls, in minutes, for a particular business\n\nII. The number of winning lottery tickets in a set of $10$ tickets\n\nIII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $0$ and $15$ at random",
      "worked_solution": "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled using an exponential distribution. Calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next call occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of winning lottery tickets is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number obtained is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
