    "q-96681": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96681",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time it takes for a taxi to reach any address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nII. The time it takes for a taxi to reach a particular address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nIII. The number of defective electronic components produced in a factory every week",
      "worked_solution": "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow an exponential distribution. The times needed to reach different destinations will likely have different average rates.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Journeys along a particular route can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time needed to reach the destination.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective components is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
